    "case_id": "710",
    "case_summary": "A 53-year-old woman presented with a 3-year history of a lump on the posterior aspect of a right knee joint. The patient had no underlying diseases. She complained of a lump growing at the posterior aspect of her right knee joint, along with pain and discomfort while walking or standing with the knee joint extended.\n\n- Range of right knee motion (ROM): 10-130°- No internal swelling of the knee joint - No tenderness of medial or lateral joint line- Normal Lachman test results- Normal valgus and varus stress tests- 3 x 3 cm sized palpable lump at posterior aspect of right knee joint\n\n- Radiographic findings:  * Normal knee joint and lower limb alignments  * Mild osteoarthritis (Kellgren-Lawrence grade 1) in both knee joints- MRI findings:  * Multi-lobulated cyst measuring 3.3 cm × 3.0 cm × 3.9 cm at popliteal fossa  * Popliteal artery running on anteromedial side of the cyst  * No unusual findings in ligament, cartilage, and meniscus  Image Title: Preoperative MRI viewsImage Description: Shows multi-lobulated popliteal cyst in sagittal, axial and coronal views, located next to posterior aspect of popliteal artery",
